-- #1554: the DISCRIMINATING control for the import-scoped constructor overlay
-- (`importedCtorTypeDecls`, compiler/types/typecheck.mdk — #674 / #1111 A-2.6).
--
-- 🚨 WHY THIS FIXTURE EXISTS, since an all-clean oracle looks like a fixture that
-- tests nothing.  It was MEASURED, on two cold-built binaries from the same tree,
-- that the entire committed `ctor_scope_*` corpus — 15 `llvm_fixtures_modules/`
-- projects, `eval_typed_modules_fixtures/ctor_scope_load_order`, and
-- `engine_fixtures/ctor_scope_local_ctor_beats_stdlib_import` — is BYTE-IDENTICAL
-- on stdout, stderr, exit code AND `check --json` when `importedCtorTypeDecls` is
-- stubbed to `[]`.  Not one of them can fail when the mechanism is removed.  This
-- file is the shape that CAN: stubbing the overlay makes this project emit a
-- spurious `W-NONEXHAUSTIVE` against the empty oracle below, and this gate reds.
--
-- THREE things have to line up, and every existing ctor_scope fixture misses at
-- least one:
--
--  1. THE OBSERVABLE IS A DIAGNOSTIC, NOT A VALUE.  `main` prints `7` then `1` with
--     the overlay ON and with it OFF — identically.  A value-golden corpus
--     (`llvm_fixtures_modules/`, whose gate diffs `entry.eval.golden` byte-for-byte)
--     is structurally incapable of seeing this class.  Hence `analyze_project_
--     fixtures/`, which pins per-file diagnostics.
--
--  2. THE LOAD ORDER MUST BE ADVERSE.  The data-universe pool is appended in LOAD
--     order and `buildOracle`'s tables are bare-name FIRST-wins (`oracleMap =
--     omFromPairs (reverseL pairs)`, frontend/exhaust.mdk), so the overlay only
--     decides anything when the WANTED module is loaded SECOND.  Every
--     `llvm_fixtures_modules/ctor_scope_*` fixture imports its wanted module FIRST
--     (e.g. `ctor_scope_ctor_name` takes `amodd` before the colliding `zmodd`), so
--     the universe's own order already lands on the right answer with the overlay
--     gone.  Here `aacfg` is imported first and `zzcfg` — the one this file means —
--     second, so the two orders disagree.
--
--  3. IT MUST REACH THE ORACLE, NOT THE SCHEME TABLES.  A-2.11
--     (`applyCtorScopeOverrides`) and A-2.12 (`applyRecordScopeOverrides`) are a
--     FIFTH layer sitting ABOVE the overlay: they re-point COLLIDED constructor and
--     record names at the declaration this module's import scope selects, which is
--     why value answers no longer move when the overlay is removed.  The
--     exhaustiveness oracle has NO such override — it is seeded once from a flat
--     list at `seedCheckRun (prog ++ importedCtorTypeDecls prog … ++ accData)` — so
--     it is the one place the overlay still decides an answer.  A `match` is
--     therefore mandatory here; `ctor_scope_load_order`, which does get the load
--     order right, has none.
--
-- EXPECTED RESULT, DERIVED BY HAND (not captured — this corpus has no regen script,
-- and capturing from a stubbed engine would enshrine the bug): `Cfg` in this file
-- can only mean `zzcfg.Cfg`, the sole `Cfg` this file imports, whose sole
-- constructor is `CZ`.  The single-arm `match` is therefore EXHAUSTIVE, `f v` is
-- `7`, `aaf 1` is `1`, and every file in the graph publishes ZERO diagnostics.
-- With the overlay stubbed the oracle instead reads `aacfg.Cfg`'s ctor set and
-- reports `non-exhaustive match of 'Cfg'. Missing case: 'CA'` — a warning about a
-- constructor this file cannot name.
import aacfg.{aaf}
import zzcfg.{Cfg, CZ}

v : Cfg
v = CZ

f : Cfg -> Int
f x = match x
  CZ => 7

main : <IO> Unit
main =
  let _ = println (f v)
  println (aaf 1)
